<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Sources of Prayer" cic="CCC 2650-2696" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Section 1: Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<p>Answer each question in your own words. Consult the lesson if you need to.</p>

<ol>
<li>What are the four principal sources of Christian prayer identified in the lesson and the Catechism (CCC 2650-2696)?</li>
<li>What is Lectio Divina? Name and briefly describe its four steps.</li>
<li>Why is the Eucharist called &laquo;the source and summit of the Christian life,&#8202;&raquo; and what does this mean for our prayer?</li>
<li>What do the theological virtues &mdash; faith, hope, and charity &mdash; have to do with prayer?</li>
<li>What does it mean to &laquo;pray without ceasing&raquo; (1 Thess 5:17), and how is this possible in a busy life?</li>
<li>Who were the Desert Fathers, and what was their contribution to the tradition of prayer?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Section 2: For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly and at length. This is for your eyes, not for grading.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Describe your current relationship with the Bible. Do you read it regularly? If not, what obstacles stand in the way? If yes, how do you approach it &mdash; as a textbook, as literature, or as the living Word of God?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be honest. There is no right or wrong answer here, only what is true for you. Naming the obstacle is the first step toward overcoming it.</p>
