<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Read Psalm 51 slowly. David prays this psalm after committing adultery and murder &mdash; not sins most of us have committed, but the posture of total honesty before God applies to every sin. Write down a verse or phrase that resonates with you. Why did it strike you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">God does not want polished prayers; he wants honest ones. What would it feel like to bring your real self &mdash; failures and all &mdash; to prayer?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. How do you participate in the Mass? Are you actively engaged, or do you attend more out of habit and obligation? If the latter troubles you, do not be ashamed: the Mass is deep, and many of us need help learning to enter it. What is one thing you could do to participate more consciously?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Concrete suggestions: arrive five minutes early, read the readings beforehand, follow the Eucharistic Prayer with attention, or choose one phrase from the liturgy to carry with you through the week.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. St. Paul tells us to &laquo;pray without ceasing.&raquo; Choose one ordinary activity you do every day &mdash; making coffee, walking to work, brushing your teeth, waiting for the bus. How could that activity become a prayer? What would change inside you if you began to offer it to God?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Prayer is not a separate compartment of life; it is the soul's breathing. The small offerings prepare the heart for the great ones.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
